WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:08.540
[CLASSICAL MUSIC]

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:18.460
We've discussed the big five traits: extroversion, that's sensitivity to positive emotion.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.440
Neuroticism: it's sensitivity to negative emotion.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.640
Not all negative emotions. Mostly fear, anxiety, and emotional pain seem to load on neuroticism.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.900
Disgust, which is another negative emotion.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:38.820
It seems to be more associated [COUGHS] with conscientiousness, particularly its orderliness aspect.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.300
Agreeableness, conscientiousness, and openness.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.920
We're going to talk about agreeableness today.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.020
Agreeableness is a very difficult personality dimension to understand, I think.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:56.120
Partly because it's difficult to dissociate from neuroticism, and as well from extroversion.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.380
Because agreeable people like you, and so that kind of sounds like extroversion.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.940
And disagreeable people sound like they're hard to get along with. And they sort of are.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.920
But people who are high in neuroticism are hard to get along with too.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.140
And they tend to be volatile and irritable.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:23.620
And so most of the time, if you're engaged in a contentious issue with someone, and emotions flare, it usually has more to do with trait neuroticism than with disagreeableness per se.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:29.680
So what I'm going to do is try to describe to you what the agreeable trait is, on both of its dimensions.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:39.080
And also to lay out the pros and cons of existence on that normal distribution trait at more or less every point.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:49.500
Because I think, the way I look at it anyways, is, of all the traits, agreeableness is the one that seems to come with the most marked positive and negative aspects.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.820
Features, let's say, so we don't confuse it with aspects.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.180
The most positive and negative features at each point on the distribution.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:59.460
It seems to be a very, very complex dimension.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:09.140
So, I'll read you some of the questions from the Big Five Aspects Scale and that will give you an initial rule-of-thumb estimate about whether or not you're agreeable or disagreeable.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.280
And so here are some of the questions.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:19.260
Imagine that you're answering these for yourself on a scale from one to five, strongly disagree to strongly agree.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.920
So the first question is:

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.080
I'm not interested in other people's problems.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.160
So, if you are interested in other people's problems, that tilts you towards agreeableness.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.140
Agreeableness is divided into compassion and politeness.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.060
Which also sound like very positive things, right?

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.960
Because everyone wants to be compassionate, and everyone wants to be polite.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:49.260
And so you might say, "Well, is that a virtue? Are those virtues, with the other end being actually negative, to be not compassionate, not polite."

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.080
It's certainly worded that way.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:56.740
And that's actually a mistake, because we know that these traits are normally distributed, roughly speaking, right?

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:04.160
And that that means that there has to be positive and negative features at every single position on the distribution.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:24.160
And so to make the pre-supposition, for example, that being extroverted is better than being introverted, or that being emotionally stable is necessarily better than being neurotic, is to make a kind of confusion of moral obligation with trait position.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:30.860
You have to assume that there's advantages and disadvantages all the way along, or the distribution wouldn't have set itself up that way.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.680
Especially because these things seem to be biologically instantiated traits.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:35.520
So

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:36.380
Anyways

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:44.060
If you're interested in other people's problems, they like to unburden themselves to you, you care about them: that's a mark of compassion.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:51.340
If you're more or less indifferent to other people's stupid problems and you wish they'd just get on with it, then you're less compassionate.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:57.820
You're harsher and more, well, at the extreme, more callous.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.940
Ah, let's see.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:03.380
Respect authority.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.000
That's politeness.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.580
That's part of agreeableness.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.740
Feel others' emotions.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:18.880
Compassion.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.940
Inquire about others' wellbeing.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:23.500
Compassion.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.160
Can't be bothered with others' needs.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.280
Take advantage of others.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.880
That's disagreeable, obviously.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.860
Sympathize with others' feelings.'

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.540
Avoid imposing my will on others.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.320
Wait for others to lead the way.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.600
Okay, I think all of those are associated with the trait agreeableness or disagreeableness.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.660
So let's think about this for a minute.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.860
So I'm going to tell you how I conceptualize agreeableness.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.900
The first thing you want to know is that women are more agreeable than men.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.740
About half a standard deviation.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:11.440
And that's approximately enough so that if you took a random male and a random female out of the population, and you tried to guess who was more agreeable, and you guessed the female, you'd be right about 60% of the time.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:19.100
But what's interesting about that, and this is something also to keep in mind, about normal distributions, you know.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.820
Imagine you have normal distribution, so that most people are in the middle.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:27.080
And then you have another normal distribution, male and female, and mostly they overlap.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:32.120
But, you see, out here, and out here, they don't overlap at all.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:40.780
And so, even though on average, men and women aren't that much different in terms of their levels of agreeableness by the group,

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.040
if you go out and you look at the extremes, they're very different.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.200
So all of the most agreeable people are women, and all of the most disagreeable people are men.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.320
And the thing is, the extremes are often what matter, rather than what's in the middle.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.280
And so one of the ways that's reflected in society, by the way, is there's way more men in prison.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.800
And the best personality predictor of being in prison is to be low in agreeableness.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.300
It makes you callous.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.600
Now, you might think, "What's the opposite of compassion and politeness?"

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:14.280
And the answer to that is, I think it's best conceptualized as a trading game.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.140
So let's say that we're going to play repeated trading games.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:24.960
And if you're very agreeable, then you're going to bargain harder on my behalf than you're going to bargain on your own behalf.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.780
Whereas if you're very disagreeable, you're going to do the reverse.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.740
You're going to think, "I'm in this trading game for me, and you're going to take care of your own interests."

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:40.500
Where an agreeable person is going to say, "No, no, at worst this has to be 50-50, but I'd like to help you every way I can."

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.880
Okay, so you kind of understand that.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:49.180
Now the advantage to being agreeable, then, is that you're good in teams and you're very much likely to give other people credit.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:54.280
The down side of being agreeable is that you're not good at putting forward your own interests.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:07:00.740
And so one of the things that predicts salary across time, for example, is agreeableness, and it predicts it negatively.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:07.100
And so it's part of the reason why women get paid less than men, and this is something for the women in the class to really listen to.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.340
Because how you get paid across time depends on a very large number of things, right?

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:17.080
It depends on your skills and your abilities and your position and your social network and all of that.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:20.400
But the other thing it depends on is whether or not you actually go ask for money.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.440
Or maybe that you don't even ask.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.240
Because actually, you don't ask for money.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.060
You tell people that you need to be paid more or something they don't like will happen.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.500
And I don't mean as a threat.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:34.820
I mean that you have to be willing, when you're negotiating, to have an alternative.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.440
You go talk to your boss, who isn't going to give you money, because everyone wants money, right?

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:39.820
It's a competitive game.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:50.380
You're going to have to go there and say, "Look, here's what I do. Here's why it's useful. Here's why you have to give me more money. And this is my opportunities if you don't."

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:57.540
And then, you're not taking your boss's money anyway, because it's very frequently the case that he's working for a whopping big company.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:03.360
But he needs an excuse to give you money because everyone's asking for money all the time.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.660
And so you have to put your case forward powerfully and disagreeably.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:16.840
Now, you don't want to do it too disagreeably because then he's gonna think that you're a son of a bitch and maybe he's not gonna give you anything and maybe you'll get fired for being mouthy, and all of that.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.420
And that certainly happens to people who are too disagreeable.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:21.140
You gotta get the balance right.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:22.520
But it's definitely the case.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:31.760
And the other thing that happens to women that's also worth noting, and this is probably because they're higher in negative emotion, is they tend to underestimate their own utility in business settings.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:43.500
Right, because if you're trying to evaluate what you're like, and you're more tilted toward negative emotion, then the things that you do that are wrong are gonna stand out more on the foreground than the things that you do that are right.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:54.720
So if you go into a negotiation, and you're uncertain already, because you have self doubts, and then you're agreeable in the negotiation, what's going to happen is that you're not going to win as often.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:02.400
And winning, in a business setting, or in a career development setting, means more opportunity for promotion and more revenue generated.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:13.800
Now, the downside of that, of course, is as you climb the business hierarchy, you also have to take on more responsibility, and that responsibility is sometimes unpleasant as well, especially to people who are agreeable.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.560
Because you're not necessarily liked if you're in a position of authority.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.780
And agreeable people really like to be liked.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:27.900
It's their primary motivator because they're concerned about the maintenance of intimate, positive relationships.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.140
That also makes them conflict avoidant.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:39.180
Okay so now, you guys can think about this, but I'll tell you why I think the personality differences between men and women exist.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:46.200
Now, these are speculative hypotheses, but they're reasonably well documented by the relevant literature.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.460
So let's think about it.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:51.020
The first thing we might think about is: what's the difference between men and women?

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:52.360
How do they differ?

00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:01.320
Well the first thing we might observe is that if you look at personality differences between pre-pubescent boys and girls, they're not very large.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.140
Boys and girls don't differ in terms of their trait neuroticism, for example.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:13.700
What happens is that, when puberty kicks in, women's trait neuroticism rises and it stays higher than men for the rest of their life.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:19.140
And this is why you see this reflected in the different kinds of psychopathology that beset the two sexes.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:27.540
So men are over-represented in alcoholism, drug abuse, anti-social personality, and a host of learning disorders as well as attention deficit disorder.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.400
And women are over-represented in depression and anxiety, primarily.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:34.700
That seems to be tightly associated with higher levels of trait neuroticism.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:40.740
Because if you're at the 95th percentile or higher, let's say, in trait neuroticism,

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:45.540
there isn't much difference between that and being somewhat prone to depression and anxiety.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:51.220
And because the curves overlap, the curves aren't identical,

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:59.140
the normal distributions aren't identical for men and women, you tilt the women's curve to the right towards higher levels of neuroticism.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:04.120
You go out and you look for the person in twenty who has the highest levels of negative emotion.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:06.700
It's much more likely to be female than male.

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.760
Okay, so let's see if we can figure out why.

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:15.500
So, we're gonna tell you some basic differences between men and women and you can tell me what you think about it, if you agree or disagree.

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:16.860
Okay, first.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:21.100
Size differential emerges between men and women at puberty, right?

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:24.280
Because boys and girls are roughly the same size and roughly the same strength.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.920
But men get bigger at puberty, when testosterone kicks in.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.580
And more importantly, not only do they get taller and heavier, but their upper body strength is much higher.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:39.920
And that's a real issue for combat, because human beings punch, and there's other animals that do that.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:43.340
Kangaroos do that too, so we're not the only people that punch.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:47.300
But we have clubs on the ends of our arms, and so that's how we defend ourselves.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:55.280
And so if you have a lot of upper body strength, especially across the shoulders, and you're heavier, then you can step into the punch and it's a lot more devastating.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:04.280
Now it is the case that, if you look at the statistics for physical altercations in marriage, women attack their husbands more often than husbands attack their wives.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:06.540
Well, you think, "Why is that?"

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:16.300
Well, let's assume that there isn't any reason, other than both people in a relationship can get upset, and the women know that if they hit their husbands, nothing's really going to happen.

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:22.380
Right, because if you're a woman about that high, and your husband is, say, my height,

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:28.240
unless you hit me with an object or something that's sharp, the probability that you're going to do me any serious damage is pretty low.

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:29.340
You might hurt me.

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:33.540
But if I do the reverse, and hit you, and I really hit you then I might kill you.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:40.620
And so, at least one of the reasons why women can be more physically aggressive in minor ways in a relationship

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:47.200
is because everyone knows, the wife and the husband equally, that the consequence of the physical aggression is much more limited.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.380
So, men do more serious damage to women.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:53.040
But women are more aggressive in relationships.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:54.240
So that's interesting.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:57.660
So ok, so there's a body size difference that's important,

00:12:57.660 --> 00:12:59.560
a strength differential that's important.

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:01.240
Next thing, I think...

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:05.880
So let's assume that the reason that women are higher in sensitivity to negative emotion

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:09.540
is because the world is actually more dangerous to women, right?

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:12.040
Because that would be the most logical reason why there would be a

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:17.360
sex difference in something like fear sensitivity and punishment.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.720
Well first there's the danger of physical altercation.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.000
Second, there's the sexual danger.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:26.280
So women become sexually vulnerable at puberty.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:27.860
And why do I say vulnerable?

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.100
Well it's straightforward.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:32.620
It's because the cost of sex for women is way higher than it is for men.

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:35.500
Or it certainly has been throughout our evolutionary history.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:39.040
Because if a man has an unwanted sexual encounter,

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:43.980
well then he walks away and maybe he is persecuted by the the state or prosecuted by the state for it.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:48.580
But if a woman has an unwanted, unwarranted, or incautious sexual encounter,

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:50.080
and she ends up pregnant,

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:53.840
then, well, in traditional societies, you're just done.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.660
And even in modern societies that are rich like ours,

00:13:56.660 --> 00:14:01.200
you're... it's a... I don't have to go into that.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:02.560
It's big trouble.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:04.580
No matter what you do about it, it's big trouble.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:08.280
So being more nervous about that makes perfect sense.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:09.800
But then, here's the last thing.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:14.940
I think that women's nervous systems are not adapted to women.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:20.300
I think women's nervous systems are adapted to the  mother-infant dyad.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.880
Because you are not the same creature when you have an infant.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:24.680
Not at all.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:26.100
You're way more vulnerable.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:30.040
And it's partly because you have to express the vulnerability of the infant.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:31.780
And you also have to care for it.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.680
Right, so, you think about an infant, especially under nine months.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:41.340
So let's say, how are you going to be wired up if you're going to optimally care for an infant under nine months?

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:47.200
And I'm saying under nine months because women generally do the bulk of child care for infants who are under nine months old.

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:51.520
And part of the reason for that, there's a whole host of reasons, but part of the reasons for that, obviously,

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:52.740
is that they breast-feed.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:57.100
But imagine what you need to be wired up biologically in order to care for an infant.

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.940
First of all, they're very demanding.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:00.900
Right?

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.640
Because they're completely helpless.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.780
And they're demanding twenty-four hours a day.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:09.840
And it's quite an emotional load.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:12.520
And an infant under nine months is never wrong.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:13.700
Right?

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:18.340
What you do to an infant under nine months, is, when they're in distress, you always respond.

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:22.780
You never tell the infant, "Get your act together and stop whining."

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:23.280
Right?

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.560
Which you can do to a child that's eighteen months old.

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:28.560
You can start having that sort of conversation.

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:32.020
But under nine months, it's like, nothing is the infant's fault,

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.740
it's surrounded in an extraordinarily threatening world,

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:39.720
and you have to be responsive to what it needs, regardless of what you want.

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:43.060
And you have to be very sensitive to the threats that emerge in the environment.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:51.020
And so I think the price that women pay for that ability to have an intimate relationship with infants in the very earliest stages of development

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:56.560
is that their nervous systems are actually wired so that they can perform that role optimally.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:03.500
And the disadvantage to that is that having a temperament like that doesn't work that well when you're dealing with adult men.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.340
Especially when you're dealing with them in a business environment.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:08.100
Because it's not the same thing.

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:09.060
Not at all.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.140
It's a competitive environment.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:13.460
So agreeable people are compassionate and polite.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:15.160
What are disagreeable people like?

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:21.400
They're tough-minded, they're blunt, they're competitive, and they won't do a damn thing they don't want to do.

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:26.820
So it isn't exactly that they're aggressive, although they will push you the hell out of their way if you're in the way.

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:31.860
They're not volatile like you are if you're high in neuroticism.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.100
It isn't defensive aggression, it's more like predatory aggression.

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:36.940
It's dominance behavior.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:40.900
And so for someone who's highly disagreeable,

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:45.280
they look at the world as a place in which they can compete and win.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:46.780
And I'll tell you a story.

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:47.540
I have a friend.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:53.840
I gave him my personality test, the Big Five Aspect Scale that Colin DeYoung developed, in my lab.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:57.880
I knew he was a disagreeable guy.

00:16:58.680 --> 00:16:59.940
By interacting with him.

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:03.520
I mean, he's even rude to people sort of spontaneously on the street.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:04.660
I actually like him quite a bit.

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:05.600
He's very, very funny.

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:07.780
He's also very conscientious, so you can trust him.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.220
But he's disagreeable as hell.

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:17.180
So I gave him this test because I thought it would be funny, and he came out as the most disagreeable person in ten thousand.

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:18.100
[STUDENTS LAUGH]

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:25.060
Reasonable in compassion, about thirtieth percentile, but point zero-zero-one in politeness.

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:26.620
So he's extraordinarily blunt.

00:17:26.620 --> 00:17:30.060
He'll just say absolutely anything, no matter how horrible it is.

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:33.700
And he was often brought into corporations to sort of clean them up.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:40.060
So if a corporation was tilting and not doing well, they'd bring him in to find out who the useless people were and fire them.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:47.580
And I talked to him about that, because I had the mis-opportunity to have to not have graduate students in my lab, for example, that weren't performing well.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:52.360
And I find it very, very difficult, to, you know, to dress someone down, and certainly difficult to fire them.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:55.780
I just hate it because I'm actually quite an agreeable person, much to my chagrin.

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:57.680
And I asked him about that.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:01.620
And I said, "Well, what do you do? You have to fire people all the time. How do you handle that?"

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.540
He says, "Handle it? I enjoy it!"

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:04.040
[STUDENTS LAUGH]

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.280
And I thought, "Wow, that's so interesting, that someone would have that response."

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:09.080
So I said, "Well, what do you mean you enjoy it?"

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:13.920
He said, "Look, I go into these companies, and I analyze the performance of groups of people.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:19.340
Right, and in those groups there are people who are really striving, really trying hard and working, themselves,

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:23.300
really hard, and being productive. And then there's these people that are just doing nothing.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:27.720
They're completely in the way. They don't carry their weight at all. They take advantage every chance they get.

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:34.540
And they're always whining about why they can't work. It's like, I find out who they are, I call them into my office, and I tell them exactly what they've been doing.

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:38.260
It's like, hit the road, buddy. You've had your run of it."

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:41.120
And I thought, "Oh yeah, okay, fair enough."

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:47.440
Well, I can tell you,  you know, I've had situations in my lab where I had under-performing graduate students.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:52.800
And one of the things that was really awful about that was that it was really hard on the high-performing graduate students.

00:18:53.220 --> 00:19:01.240
You know, because they felt that even being in the same category as the people who weren't working hard and pulling their weight devalued what they were doing.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:02.760
You know? And that's exactly right.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:08.580
And so this is also why there's a conscientiousness trait and an agreeableness trait.

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:12.760
Because conscientious people judge you on your accomplishments, right?

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.540
They don't give a damn about your feelings.

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:15.260
Not a bit.

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:17.440
It's like, "Are you doing the work, or not?"

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:25.780
Whereas agreeable people think, "Well, you know, your mother's sick, and you've got a bunch of family problems, and we all have to take care of each other."

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:27.820
"And it's no wonder that you're having a rough time."

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:32.040
You can't say that one of those attitudes is correct and the other isn't correct.

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:33.520
You can't say that!

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:37.680
There wouldn't be those two dimensions if there wasn't something correct about both of them.

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:40.720
But you can certainly point out that often they conflict.

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:45.180
You know, and so the demand for inclusiveness and unity and care,

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:51.380
and the demand for high level performance in a hierarchical structure- they're very different orientations in the world.

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:56.480
It's complicated for people who are agreeable AND conscientious.

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:09.760
And actually, I think often, that large corporations, large institutions of any sort, run on the un-heralded labor of people who are high in agreeableness and high in conscientiousness.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.740
And they're disproportionately women.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:18.120
My experience in large institutions has been that if you want to hire someone to exploit appropriately,

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:19.440
No, not appropriately..

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:26.120
If you want to hire someone to exploit productively, you hire middle-aged women who are hyper-conscientious and who are agreeable.

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:27.980
Because they'll do everything.

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:30.240
They won't take credit for it, and they won't complain.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:31.780
And that's nasty.

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:33.560
And I think that happens all the time.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:38.160
And so one of the things you have to be careful of, if you're agreeable, is not to be exploited.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:40.620
Because you'll line up to be exploited.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:44.680
And I think the reason for that is because you're wired to be exploited by infants.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:49.300
And so, that just doesn't work so well in the actual world.

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.300
One of the things that happens very often in psychotherapy,

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:54.700
you know, people come to psychotherapy for multiple reasons.

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:57.900
But one of them is they often come because they're too agreeable.

00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:00.900
And so what they get is so-called "assertiveness training."

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:03.560
Although it's not exactly assertiveness that's being trained.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:06.920
What it is the ability to learn how to negotiate on your own behalf.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:10.960
And one of the things I tell agreeable people, especially if they're conscientious, is

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:15.140
Say what you think. Tell the truth about what you think.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.500
There's gonna be things you think that you think are nasty and harsh.

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:20.640
And they probably are nasty and harsh.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:22.820
But they're also probably true.

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:25.960
And you need to bring those up to the forefront and deliver the message.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:30.840
And it's not straightforward at all because agreeable people do not like conflict.

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:32.200
Not at all.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:33.620
They smooth the water.

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:39.240
And you can see why that is, in accordance with the hypothesis that I've been putting forward.

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:41.160
You don't want conflict around infants.

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:42.540
It's too damn dangerous.

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.020
You don't want fights to break out.

00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:48.000
You don't want anything to disturb the relative peace.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:58.660
If you're also more prone to being hurt, physically, and perhaps emotionally, you're also maybe loathe to engage in the kind of high intensity conflict that will solve problems in the short term.

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:03.400
Because it takes a lot of conflict to solve problems in the short term.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:08.360
And if that can spiral up to where it's dangerous, which it can, it gets uncontrolled,

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:12.980
it might be safer in the short term to keep the waters smooth,

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:16.120
and to not delve into those situations where conflict emerges.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:19.220
The problem with that is it's not a very good medium to long-term strategy.

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:22.980
Right, because lots of times there's things you have to talk about.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:25.260
Because they're not going to go away.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:30.840
And so partly what you do with agreeable people is, you get them to figure out- and they have a hard time with this too.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:35.380
If you ask a disagreeable person what he wants, say, or she wants, they'll tell you right away.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:38.220
They go, "This is what I want, and this is how I'm going to get it."

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:44.320
Agreeable people, especially if they're really agreeable, are so agreeable, that they often don't even know what they want.

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:51.060
Because they're so accustomed to living for other people, and to finding out what other people want, and to trying to make them comfortable, and so forth,

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:56.440
that it's harder for them to find a sense of their own desires as they move through life.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:06.520
And that's not... look, there's situations where that's advantageous, but it's certainly not advantageous if you're going to try to forge yourself a career.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:08.100
That just doesn't work at all.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:09.080
So...

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:12.260
All right.

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:15.440
What else do I want to tell you about agreeableness?

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:20.260
Well, I can tell you a little bit about the regulation of aggression.

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:30.080
One of the things I studied, especially when I was in Montreal, was the development of antisocial behavior in children and in adolescents.

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:36.140
Antisocial children, so they tend to be aggressive, antisocial children tend to turn into antisocial...

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:38.580
It's conduct disorder, technically speaking.

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:41.820
Conduct disordered children tend to turn into conduct disordered adolescents.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:45.320
And then they tend to turn into antisocial and criminal adults.

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:49.220
And so I can tell you little bit about how that progresses, I think, because it's quite interesting.

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:50.940
And it isn't what people generally think.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:57.920
So, the first thing is, if you want to be criminal, the best way to do it is to be really low in agreeableness and really low in conscientiousness.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:02.440
Because low in agreeableness means, "Things are for me and not for you.

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:06.840
And you're not going to get me to do a damn that I don't want to do, and I'll stand my ground."

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:11.540
And low in conscientiousness means you can do all the work and I'll sit back and take the benefits.

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:19.160
And so if you have someone who's really disagreeable, and really unconscientious, you have someone who's starting to border on psychopath.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:29.600
And if you add high intelligence and high emotional stability to that, then you have someone who won't work but will reap the benefits, who doesn't give a damn about you, who is assertive as hell and who's smart.

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:36.180
And a person like that's also going to be charismatic because extroverted disagreeable people are kind of narcissistic.

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:39.460
But they're... they'll put themselves forward strongly.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:43.000
And if they don't show any signs of fear, that also indicates that they're confident.

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:45.860
And it's easy for people to confuse that with competence.

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:48.120
And that's how psychopaths get away with what they're doing.

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:52.760
Although they have to move from person to person because their reputation will track them.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:59.180
So anyways, back to the development of aggression in children, the development of criminality in adults.

00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:01.100
Here's how it seem to work, at least in part.

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:05.020
It's more complicated than this, but I'll put in some of the sociological elements as well.

00:25:05.740 --> 00:25:06.240
So

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:07.640
If you take

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:09.460
children

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:16.080
and you group them together in groups defined by age,

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:22.000
So let's say you have 30 two year olds, 30 three year olds, 30 four year olds, all the way up to eighteen.

00:25:22.420 --> 00:25:31.560
And then you watch them interact, and you code their behavior for kicking, biting, fighting, and property theft,

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:36.140
then what you'll find is that the two year olds are by far the most aggressive of the lot.

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:38.920
So that's pretty interesting, you know, because you think,

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:42.820
"Well, children are naturally peaceful, and if they're aggressive it's because they learn it."

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:47.480
It's like, no, that's true for a small minority of children.

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:53.180
But there's a substantial number of children who are aggressive at two, by nature.

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:54.640
Most of them are male.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:26:01.120
Now that doesn't mean most children are like that, because they're not, even if you look at two year olds, who are the most aggressive human beings.

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:03.060
Most two year olds aren't aggressive.

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:04.300
But some of them are.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:05.920
And most of those are male.

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:10.340
Okay, so then let's say you identify this cohort of aggressive two year olds.

00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:12.120
And you track them across time.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:17.440
Track them for the next 2-4 years. Track them until they're four years old.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:25.020
What you find is the vast majority of the hyper-aggressive male two year olds get socialized perfectly well.

00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:31.260
So by the time they're four, they're temperamentally probably still more aggressive, but they become civilized little monsters.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:32.720
So other people can tolerate them.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:41.380
And that means that they've had parents, or peers, or educational experiences, that enabled them to learn how to interact productively with other kids.

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:43.600
And to bring their aggressive nature under control.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:48.300
Some of that seems to be mediated by the opportunity to engage in rough-and-tumble play.

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:52.740
And that's one of the things that we know that that helps socialize rats, for example.

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:57.900
It's vital to them, but it also seems to really be good for socializing young kids.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:03.120
And rough-and-tumble play, which is something that adult males particularly like to do with young kids, by the way,

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:06.060
not before nine months, because they're just too little,

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:13.060
but once they become ambulatory, and kind of puppy-like so that they're a little bit more robust, then you can play a lot with them.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:18.640
And you can play with them right at the edge of danger, too, which kids, they absolutely go nuts for that.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:19.500
They love that.

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:25.280
When I had little kids, I made this kind of wrestling ring out of these two couches that we had that would hook together.

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:29.400
And I'd bring them on there and, you know, toss them up in the air and catch them, you know.

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:31.740
Eight, ten feet- no, no.

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:33.220
[STUDENT LAUGHTER]

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:38.400
A foot in the air and catch them, and they'd go like this, and then, you know, I'd catch them, and they'd laugh,

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:41.000
and I'd throw them up and they're all freaked out and then they'd laugh.

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:45.560
So they're learning trust with that in the bodied way, and they're also learning,

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:51.980
and this is from stretching them out, and wrestling them, and twisting them around, and letting them pull on your hair and hit you, and all of those things,

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:56.220
they learn deep in their bones exactly what can hurt them and what doesn't.

00:27:56.220 --> 00:28:03.660
And you want to kind of push them to the edge, you know, so that they can tell the difference between what hurts and what's still within the realm of the game.

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:05.660
And you do the same thing when they're wrestling with you.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:08.280
So they learn not to, you know, awkwardly stick their thumb in your eye.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:13.200
Or do things that are actually painful like grab your lip and pull it.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:17.380
It's like, no, no, you let go of my lip, you know?

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:21.080
And so, that seems to help regulate the aggressive impulses.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:25.100
And help the child find a more appropriate embodiment.

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:29.800
You can think that what you're doing, in some sense, when you're rough-and-tumble playing with kids is teaching them how to dance.

00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:31.520
Because that is what you're doing.

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:36.080
You know? You're making them comfortable in their bodies in all of its extension.

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:41.600
And building in that kind of body fluency that you see in people who are well-situated inside themselves.

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:43.640
And so that's something to really think about, you know?

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:45.040
And it's appalling.

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:51.760
We know that the ability to engage in rough-and-tumble play among rats inhibits aggression, impulsive aggression, among rats.

00:28:51.920 --> 00:29:00.440
We also know that if you deprive rats of the opportunity to engage in rough-and-tumble play, they show pre-frontal cortical developmental deficits.

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:05.280
And manifest behaviors that are akin to attention deficit disorder, which you can then treat with Ritalin.

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:20.940
And so one of the things that's happening with boys, because they're way more dosed with attention deficit disorder medication than girls is that their natural proclivity to engage in robust and troublesome active play isn't appropriate for a school environment

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.380
where you're supposed to sit down and shut up.

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:25.160
And so the kids get hyperactive.

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:30.320
And instead of letting them out to run around until they fall over half exhausted, which is exactly what you should do,

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:40.220
you know, and you medicate them, so that their exploratory systems, the activity of which is facilitated by the dopaminergic agonist,

00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:44.440
that's the ADHD medication, suppresses the play function.

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:47.620
It's absolutely appalling. There's no excuse for it.

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:54.140
You know, but it's a good indictment of the education system, because why in the world would you take six year old kids

00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:59.940
and get them to sit without moving for five hours, unless you want them to grow up fat and stupid?

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.060
Why in the world would you train them to do that?

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:04.140
Well it's easier. That's one thing.

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:11.280
And when they're sitting there, there's nothing disruptive about the rough-and-tumble play, and that can be quite disruptive.

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:13.460
So anyways, most of these kids

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:17.100
are reasonably well socialized by the time they're the age of four,

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:18.660
using one mechanism or another.

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:24.200
They learn how to regulate their aggression and they learn how to engage in fictional play structures with other kids.

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:26.020
They learn how to cooperate and compete.

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:32.420
And the advantage to having a well-socialized disagreeable person is that they really don't let much get in their way.

00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:38.460
So if you can get a kid who's disagreeable socialized, that person can be quite the creature, you know?

00:30:38.460 --> 00:30:43.460
Because they're very forward-moving in their nature and very difficult to stop.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:50.840
But if you don't get them successfully "domesticated," tamed, roughly speaking, by the time they're four,

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:52.980
their peers reject them.

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:55.280
And that's a big problem because

00:30:55.760 --> 00:31:00.260
your job as a parent is to make your child socially desirable by the age of four.

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:03.500
You want to burn that into your brain.

00:31:03.500 --> 00:31:05.180
Because people don't know that.

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:06.300
That's your job.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:10.260
And here's why, it's easy if you think about it carefully.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:16.700
So you imagine, you've got a three year old child, so halfway through that initial period of socialization,

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:18.460
and you take that child out in public.

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:21.260
Okay, what do you want for the child?

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:22.620
Who care about you?

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:24.280
What do you want for the child?

00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:29.220
You want the child to be able to interact with other children and adults,

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:37.960
so that the children are welcoming and smile and want to play with him or her, and the adults are happy to see the child and treat him or her properly.

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:43.420
And if your child's a horrible little monster because you're afraid of disciplining or you don't know how to do that properly,

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:51.340
then what they're going to do is, they're going to experience nothing but rejection from other children, and false smiles from other parents and adults.

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:58.760
So then you're throwing the child out there into a world where every single face that they see is either hostile or lying.

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:04.240
And that's not something that's going to be particularly conducive to the mental health or the wellbeing of your child.

00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:10.360
If your child can learn a couple simple rules of behavior, like don't interrupt adults when they're talking too much,

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:15.460
and pay attention, and try not to hit the other kids over the head with the truck any more than is absolutely necessary,

00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:22.760
and share and play properly, then when they meet other kids, the kids are going to try out a few little play routines on them,

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:27.660
and that's going to go well, and then they're going to go off, and socialize each other for the rest of their lives.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:35.380
Because that's what happens, is that from four years old onwards, the primary socialization with children takes place among other children.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:38.260
And so if the kids don't get in on that early,

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:42.680
they don't move into that developmental spiral upwards and they're left behind.

00:32:42.680 --> 00:32:48.740
And you can imagine how terrible that is, because a four year old will not play with another four year old who's two.

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:53.380
But a five year old certainly will not play with a five year old who's two.

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:56.460
Right, because the gap is just starting to become unbelievably large.

00:32:56.460 --> 00:33:00.980
And so the kids start out behind, and then the peers leave them behind,

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:05.620
and then those kids are alienated and outside the peer group for the rest of their life.

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:08.940
Those are the ones that grow up to be long-term anti-social.

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:12.260
They're already aggressive. It doesn't dip down.

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:15.680
Now what happens to normal boys, roughly speaking,

00:33:15.800 --> 00:33:18.680
imagine the aggressive two year old types,

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:24.640
they get socialized, so their level of aggression goes down, and then they hit puberty, and testosterone kicks in, and bang!

00:33:24.740 --> 00:33:26.480
Levels of aggression go back up.

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:31.320
And so that's why males are criminals between the ages roughly of sixteen and about twenty-five.

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:34.740
And it matches the creativity curve, by the way. It's so cool.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:40.180
If you look at the spike of creativity among men, sixteen to twenty-five, it starts to go down,

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:42.840
criminality matches that absolutely perfectly.

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:43.880
So that's quite cool.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:50.520
So the testosterone levels raise the average level of aggression among men,

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:54.620
more dominance than aggression, actually, and testosterone is by no means all bad,

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:58.780
and then it starts to decrease around age twenty-five or twenty-six, which is usually, with men,

00:33:59.140 --> 00:34:10.160
stop staying up late at night, stop drinking as much, develop a full time career, and take on the burdens and responsibilities and opportunities that are associated with a long-term partner and family.

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:25.840
So that's the development of what I would call predatory aggression, because I also think that the agreeableness distribution is probably something like predatory aggression,

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:27.840
versus maternal sympathy.

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:29.380
It's something like that.

00:34:29.380 --> 00:34:35.220
So if you look at other mammals that are predators, because we're predators as well as prey animals,

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:37.220
if you look at other animals like bears,

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:40.780
the male bear has absolutely nothing to do with the raising of the infants.

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:45.440
In fact, the female bears will keep the male the hell away because he's likely to kill the infants,

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:46.640
and maybe even to eat them.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:54.760
So there's no maternality at all in solitary, male, mammalian predators.

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:58.800
Now it depends on how social they are, but roughly speaking, that's the situation.

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:01.940
Whereas with human beings, males are quite maternal.

00:35:02.420 --> 00:35:07.800
So, but anyways, I think the extreme of agreeableness,

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:10.820
on the low end, so disagreeableness, is predation.

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:14.260
And the extreme on the upper end is maternal caring.

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:16.980
And those two things compete, right?

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:20.740
Obviously, it's very difficult to be both of those at the same time.

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:24.700
And so men, of course, in the wild, so to speak, are...

00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:26.260
Very, very few women hunt

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:29.780
in modern societies or in archaic societies.

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:35.680
And you can also understand why that it because hunting actually requires hurting something, killing it.

00:35:35.680 --> 00:35:41.840
And it's usually something that's not very impressed about being hurt or killed, and it will emit a lot of distress while it's happening.

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:48.040
And so for anybody who's compassionate, who's got compassion as one of the fundamental elements of their temperament,

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:50.440
that's something they're just not going to be able to tolerate at all.

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:55.840
In the evolutionary landscape, because that's really what we're talking about,

00:35:55.840 --> 00:36:00.120
there's tension behind the development of different modes of being in the world.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:05.420
And if you're good at one thing, that sometimes means that you can't be good at the other thing at the same time.

00:36:06.340 --> 00:36:06.840
So...

00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:13.560
I was going to have a guest speaker for you last week, but the person I had invited in had some familial trouble,

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:14.700
so that didn't work out.

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:20.620
She's a graduate student of mine and one of the things that we'd be working on in my lab, generally speaking,

00:36:20.620 --> 00:36:26.220
is the delineation of the relationship between personality traits and political belief.

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:30.580
And so I told you a little bit about the things that distinguish liberals from conservatives, right?

00:36:30.580 --> 00:36:34.560
The conservatives are high in conscientiousness, especially orderliness,

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:36.580
and low in trait openness.

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:42.680
And so that means a conservative is someone who puts someone in boxes, puts the boxes in order,

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:44.980
and doesn't like them to be messed up.

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:53.160
So they like the borders between things to remain determinate and what would you say,

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:54.400
inviolable.

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:58.600
And I think that's true at every level from the conceptual all the way up to the political.

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:01.620
I think that the fundamental political question

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:06.060
and I think this is why the temperaments align across these political dimensions,

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.780
is whether the borders between things should be open or closed.

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:13.440
We see this reflected worldwide right now in then arguments about immigration, right?

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:16.440
Because the liberal types are saying, "Open the gates."

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:20.540
And the conservatives are saying, "Wait, you don't know what you're inviting in."

00:37:20.540 --> 00:37:22.140
And you might say, "Well, who's right?"

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:25.020
And the answer is you don't know.

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:26.140
That's the thing.

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:32.520
Because sometimes the right answer is, "Open the gates, these are interesting people, we could trade with them, we could learn from each other."

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:38.740
And sometimes the answer is, "Don't bother. What's going to come in is going to wipe you out and kill you, and really do it."

00:37:38.800 --> 00:37:40.520
I mean, so, here's an example.

00:37:41.340 --> 00:37:45.620
This is why I think orderliness is associated with disgust sensitivity.

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:49.160
And it's one of the determining factors of conservative political belief.

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:51.760
What happened when the Europeans came to North America?

00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:54.300
What happened when the Spaniards came to North America?

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:58.180
95% of the Native Americans died.

00:37:58.840 --> 00:37:59.340
Why?

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:02.400
Because the Spaniards brought illness.

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:08.980
Smallpox, measles, chicken pox, all these things that the Native Americans had absolutely no resistance to.

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:12.300
There were hardly any diseases at all in North and South America.

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:18.040
The Spaniards showed up, within 15-20 years, 19 out of 20 of the Native Americans were dead.

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:21.760
You never know what people are bringing with them.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:28.420
And so what that means is that how should you respond to people who are outside of your circle of familiarity?

00:38:28.860 --> 00:38:30.060
Well the answer is

00:38:30.500 --> 00:38:33.840
One, they might kill you, in all sorts of ways .

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:38.440
Two, they might bring with them things to trade that are of inestimable value.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:42.580
So you're stuck. It's like, how the hell are you going to reconcile that problem?

00:38:42.580 --> 00:38:46.040
And the answer is, well we reconcile it temperamentally, roughly speaking.

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:56.280
So half the people are temperamentally wired up to say, "No, no, no! Let's keep the damn boxes closed. Took a long time to pack everything in there and to get it into order."

00:38:56.460 --> 00:39:08.420
And the liberals say, "Well wait a minute. You don't know if you've got things in the right boxes to begin with. The things that you're keeping in there were getting stale and old. And maybe we need some new ideas and new people to rejuvenate the situation."

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:11.240
That's political discussion.

00:39:11.240 --> 00:39:17.120
And the political discussion has to proceed because there's no way of solving that problem except by discussing it.

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:20.720
Well, how does that relate to agreeableness?

00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:22.700
We also looked at political correctness.

00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:25.040
And so here's an interesting thing.

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:27.180
Purely from a scientific perspective.

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:28.940
You might ask yourself,

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:36.440
If you talk about political correctness, one of the things that the people who tend to be labeled as politically correct say is

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:38.720
there's no such thing as political correctness.

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:46.120
It's just a pejorative label that people who are opposed to those views impose on a set of beliefs to demonize it.

00:39:46.540 --> 00:39:49.700
Perfectly reasonable objection, and you never know, it might be true.

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:52.720
But the thing is, psychometrically, you can solve that problem,

00:39:52.720 --> 00:39:57.280
and you solve it the same way that you solve the problem of what constitutes human personality.

00:39:57.280 --> 00:39:58.940
So this is the way we tried to solve it.

00:39:58.940 --> 00:40:01.940
You can think about it methodologically, because that's how you should think about it.

00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:11.840
The first thing we did was collect a very large number of statements from press accounts that seemed to indicate what people generally referred to as political correctnes.

00:40:11.860 --> 00:40:15.460
So we had a small team of people combing media reports

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:24.500
to come out with opinions or attitudes that looked like they were characteristic of the differentiation between politically correct people and people who aren't politically correct.

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:27.120
So we got about four hundred statements like that.

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:35.620
And what you want to do is, you imagine that there's a core set of beliefs and statements that people are defining in a particular way.

00:40:35.680 --> 00:40:39.280
And you're trying to get a handle on what that is, and even if it exists.

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:41.020
What you do is over-sample it.

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:48.760
If you can find questions that might even tangentially be related to the phenomena in question,

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:50.100
you include those.

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:52.720
Because the statistics will take care of the excess.

00:40:53.060 --> 00:40:56.440
Okay, so then what you do is you take your four hundred items, roughly speaking,

00:40:56.800 --> 00:41:02.960
and you get people to register the degree to which they agree or disagree with them, get a thousand people to do that.

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:05.780
And then you subject that to a factor analysis.

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:10.520
And what the factor analysis does is tell how those questions clump.

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:12.260
Now, they might not clump.

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:16.560
So for example, you could do a factor analysis of a set of questions, and you could find that there are

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:20.440
two hundred factors, let's say you have four hundred items.

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:21.520
There's two hundred factors.

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:25.580
And none of them... there's no big set of questions that are clumping together.

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:35.500
And you say, "Well, there's no evidence here that there is a single, underlying phenomena that unifies those questions that you can reasonably characterize with a name."

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:37.320
Well that isn't what we found.

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:40.220
We found that there were two dimensions of political correctness.

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:42.700
One of them looked like liberalism,

00:41:43.100 --> 00:41:49.420
except that the people who were politically correct, in addition to being liberal, were very high in trait agreeableness.

00:41:51.480 --> 00:41:55.080
And agreeableness has almost nothing to do with the classic liberal-conservative divide.

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:59.920
It's weakly related in that conservatives are more compassionate than liberals,

00:41:59.920 --> 00:42:03.760
Sorry, liberals are more compassionate than conservatives, the difference isn't huge,

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:08.100
and conservatives are more polite than liberals, and the difference isn't huge.

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:09.960
So those are the two aspects of agreeableness.

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:12.700
If you put them together, they cancel each other out.

00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:15.920
And so on average, conservatives and liberals don't differ in agreeableness.

00:42:15.980 --> 00:42:22.140
But political correctness did clump together into two categories,

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:25.320
PC liberalism, we call it PC authoritarianism.

00:42:25.480 --> 00:42:27.440
The PC liberals were high in openness,

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:31.740
high in verbal intelligence, and high in agreeableness.

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:34.520
And the second group was PC authoritarians.

00:42:34.720 --> 00:42:41.920
And they were also high in agreeableness, but they were high in orderliness, and the correlation with that was negative in relationship to verbal intelligence.

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:47.640
So we found that there were two categories of political correctness, that it does in fact exist,

00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:50.160
but that it's a very unstable construct because

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:57.760
factor one, which was PC liberalism, and factor two, which was PC authoritarianism, were only correlated at 0.1.

00:42:58.120 --> 00:43:05.340
And so what it indicates, and this is our prediction, roughly speaking, from the lab, is to the degree that there's unity, so to speak, on the politically correct left,

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.020
it'll fragment into two groups.

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:18.180
One will be the PC liberals, and the other will be the PC authoritarians, because although they're united by their tendency to agreeableness, they're not united by other temperamental traits, nor in their core beliefs.

00:43:18.180 --> 00:43:22.160
So the PC authoritarian types, for example, are very obsessed with language control.

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:27.560
And it's funny that it's agreeableness. I've really been thinking about that.

00:43:27.560 --> 00:43:33.600
You know, again, this is a hypothesis in development, but I think what's happening is that

00:43:33.680 --> 00:43:40.680
you know, your temperamental proclivity allows you to lay out a kind of radical simplification on the world.

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:42.960
That's part of the advantage of having a temperament.

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:46.000
So if you're a conscientious person, the world is a place to go out there and work.

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:50.840
If you're an open person, the world is a place to go out there to discover new ideas and do artistic things.

00:43:50.840 --> 00:43:54.940
If you're an agreeable person, the world's a place to go out there and establish intimate relationships.

00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:57.020
So they're simplifying

00:43:59.160 --> 00:44:02.560
perspectives, and simplifying personalities.

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:07.760
They're the manner in which you're adapted to a particular niche.

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:21.200
I think what you see in agreeableness in relationship to political belief is a proclivity for people to divide the world into defenseless infants and predatory oppressors.

00:44:21.200 --> 00:44:27.740
And that that's blasted forward onto the political landscape, and things are conceptualized along that temperamental variable.

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:33.860
Anyways, that's where we're at with regards to the analysis of political belief.

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:42.760
Well, we've got three minutes left, and I'm wondering if anyone has a particularly intelligent question or failing that, any question at all?

00:44:43.180 --> 00:44:46.860
Because I told you a lot of things that are, I would say contentious.

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:49.160
But I also think they're very much worth knowing.

00:44:49.460 --> 00:44:51.120
So I, yes?

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:58.940
Did you say that high in agreeableness is the simplified perspective of the mother-infant dynamic?

00:44:58.940 --> 00:45:01.240
Yeah, that's what it looks like to me, yeah.

00:45:02.780 --> 00:45:04.840
Projected onto the world, period. Yeah.

00:45:06.500 --> 00:45:13.900
So what would you say is the best way to go about, knowing that we have these simplified ways of looking at the world, what then?

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:19.300
It's really useful to investigate the viewpoints of people who have opposing views to yours.

00:45:20.320 --> 00:45:23.600
Because they'll tell you things, not only will they tell you things you don't know,

00:45:24.140 --> 00:45:27.300
they'll also tell you how to see the world in ways that you don't see it.

00:45:28.060 --> 00:45:31.640
And they'll also have skills that you don't have, that you could develop.

00:45:32.080 --> 00:45:40.460
So for example, if you're an introverted person, it's very useful to watch an extroverted person, because the extroverted person has ways of being in the social world that aren't natural to you,

00:45:40.460 --> 00:45:43.080
that you can use to improve your toolkit.

00:45:43.300 --> 00:45:50.940
And if you're disagreeable, one of the best things to do with disagreeable people, especially if that's alienating them from other people, for example, because it can,

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:55.520
you know, people treat you like you're a selfish, arrogant son-of-a-bitch, and maybe that's because you are.

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:57.380
It's like, okay, so what do you do about that?

00:45:57.540 --> 00:46:03.420
One of the most promising "treatments," let's say,

00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:06.940
is get the person to do something for someone else once a day.

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:09.380
Just as a practice, and learn how to do it.

00:46:09.380 --> 00:46:14.500
Maybe you can wake the circuit up, you know, if you think that it's lying dormant in you, which is probably right.

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:18.220
You know, I think we have a very wide range of propensities within us.

00:46:18.340 --> 00:46:19.640
Some are switched on.

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:22.420
Genetic propensity. Some are switched on.

00:46:22.420 --> 00:46:28.660
But I think that if you put yourself in the right situation or walk yourself through the right exercises, you can switch some of these other things on as well.

00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:31.920
But it takes work and dedication and discipline to do it.

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:35.500
So actively confront the things which are not a part of our personality?

00:46:35.500 --> 00:46:43.380
Yes. Well, I would say, generally speaking, if you want to adapt yourself properly to life, you should find a niche in the environment that corresponds with your temperament.

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:48.160
Right? You shouldn't work at cross-purposes to your temperament, because it's just too damn difficult.

00:46:48.400 --> 00:46:50.100
But having done that,

00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:57.980
then you should work on developing the skills and viewpoints that exist in the space opposite to your personality.

00:46:58.220 --> 00:47:00.340
Because that's where you're fundamentally underdeveloped.

00:47:00.620 --> 00:47:10.360
That way you can extend out your temperamental capability across a wider range, and to me that's roughly equivalent as bringing a richer toolkit to each situation.

00:47:11.020 --> 00:47:14.460
You know, so if you're hyper-extroverted, you should probably learn to shut up at parties now and then.

00:47:14.880 --> 00:47:18.080
And listen just to see what's going on, to see if you can manage it.

00:47:18.400 --> 00:47:26.920
And if you're introverted, well then you should learn how to speak in public, and to learn how to go to parties without hiding in the corner and saying nothing to anyone.

00:47:27.080 --> 00:47:31.160
And if you're agreeable, then you need to learn how to be disagreeable, so people can't push you around.

00:47:31.400 --> 00:47:36.500
And if you're disagreeable, you need to learn how to be agreeable so that you're not an evil son-of-a-bitch, you know?

00:47:36.900 --> 00:47:40.440
And the same thing applies even in the conscientious domain.

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:46.080
If you're too conscientious, you need to learn to relax and let go a little bit.

00:47:46.080 --> 00:47:59.160
And if you're unconscientious, it's time, like, get out and Google "calendar," man, and start scheduling your day, right, and beat yourself on the back of the head with a stick until you're disciplined enough so that you can actually stick to something for some length of time.

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:05.500
And not living in absolute squalor, which is something that would characterize someone who is very disorderly, for example.

00:48:05.500 --> 00:48:10.120
Because they just don't notice. It doesn't bother them, disorder.

00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:16.820
It's like, maybe they can see it, but it doesn't have any emotional valence and so it doesn't have any motivational significance.

00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:26.120
The other thing you might want to think about, too, if you're choosing a partner, is try not to choose someone who's too distant from you on the temperamental variables.

00:48:26.120 --> 00:48:28.360
Because you're going to have a hard time bridging the gap.

00:48:28.880 --> 00:48:32.620
You know, it's hard for an introverted person and an extroverted person to co-exist.

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:38.740
And it's really hard for an orderly person and a disorderly person to co-exist because they will drive each other nuts.

00:48:39.040 --> 00:48:40.200
Why don't you pick up?

00:48:40.460 --> 00:48:42.380
Why are you so obsessed by it?

00:48:42.680 --> 00:48:44.180
That's the basic argument.

00:48:45.320 --> 00:48:53.740
So it's useful to know about your temperament so that you can negotiate the space with your partner as well, and I don't think you should try to find someone who's exactly the same as you,

00:48:54.100 --> 00:48:58.000
because then you don't have the benefits of the alternative viewpoint.

00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:02.760
But you've gotta watch it because you may hit irreconcilable differences of various sorts.

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:09.280
And I've seen that most particularly among couples who are high and low in openness, that's a rough one.

00:49:09.660 --> 00:49:17.720
And also high and low in conscientiousness, that's another rough one, because they just cannot see how the other person sees the world at all.

00:49:18.140 --> 00:49:20.140
Okay, I'll see you on Tuesday.

